[ { "family": "ISO 26262-5 hardware architectural metrics (SPFM, LFM, PMHF)", "formulas": [ { "name": "SPFM (Single-Point Fault Metric)", "expression": "SPFM = 1 - (lambda_SP / (lambda_total)), where lambda_total = lambda_SP + lambda_RF + lambda_S + lambda_MPF_uncovered. Equivalently: SPFM = 1 - ( sum_{F_i in SPF} lambda_F_i ) / ( sum_{all faults} lambda_F_i ), where the denominator sums over all single-point, residual, safe, and multiple-point faults whose coverage is not covered by the diagnostic coverage of the safety mechanism.", "variables": [ {"symbol": "lambda_SP", "meaning": "failure rate of single-point faults (per hour)", "units": "1/h"}, {"symbol": "lambda_RF", "meaning": "failure rate of residual faults (per hour)", "units": "1/h"}, {"symbol": "lambda_S", "meaning": "failure rate of safe faults (per hour)", "units": "1/h"}, {"symbol": "lambda_MPF_uncovered", "meaning": "failure rate of multiple-point faults whose (n-1)-th fault is not covered by any safety mechanism (per hour "1/h"} ], "worked_example": "Suppose lambda_SP = 50 FIT, lambda_RF = 20 FIT, lambda_S = 500 FIT, lambda_MPF_uncovered = 30 FIT. 1 FIT = 1e-9 /h. Numerator = (50 + 20) FIT = 70 FIT. Denominator = (50 + 20 + 500 + 30) FIT = 600 FIT. SPFM = 1 - 70/600 = 1 - 0.11667 = 0.88333, i.e. 88.33%. ASIL C/D target >= 99% per ISO 26262-5:2018 Table 5.", "authority": "ISO 26262-5:2018, Clause 8.3 (hardware architectural metrics), and Table 5 (target values)", "clause_locator": "ISO 26262-5:2018 Table 5 for target values; clause 8.3 for metric definitions", "known_uncertainty": "Some derivations exclude lambda_S from the denominator of SPFM and define it over the sum of all considered fault categories only. The form above (denominator includes lambda_S) is the common form shown in ISO 26262-5:2018 Annex C. Exact wording of the standard should be checked verbatim before any compliance use." }, { "name": "LFM (Latent Fault Metric)", "expression": "LFM = 1 - ( sum_{F_i in MPF,latent,uncovered} lambda_F_i ) / ( sum_{F_i in MPF} lambda_F_i ), where the numerator sums latent multiple-point faults whose (n-1)-th fault is not detected by any safety mechanism, and the denominator sums over ALL multiple-point faults (latent + perceived). Equivalently LFM = 1 - lambda_MPF,latent,uncovered / lambda_total_MPF.", "variables": [ {"symbol": "lambda_MPF,latent,uncovered", "meaning": "failure rate of multiple-point faults that are latent and not covered by safety mechanisms", "units": "1/h"}, {"symbol": "lambda_total_MPF", "meaning": "total failure rate of all multiple-point faults (latent + perceived), equal to lambda_MPF = lambda_MPF,latent,uncovered + lambda_MPF,latent,covered + lambda_MPF,perceived,covered + lambda_MPF,perceived,uncovered", "units": "1/h"} ], "worked_example": "Let lambda_MPF = 200 FIT. Of these, latent + uncovered = 30 FIT, latent + covered = 70 FIT, perceived + covered = 90 FIT, perceived + uncovered = 10 FIT. Numerator = 30 FIT. Denominator = (30 + 70 + 90 + 10) FIT = 200 FIT. LFM = 1 - 30/200 = 1 - 0.15 = 0.85, i.e. 85%. ASIL C/D target >= 90% per ISO 26262-5:2018 Table 5.", "authority": "ISO 26262-5:2018, Clause 8.3 and Table 5", "clause_locator": "ISO 26262-5:2018 Clause 8.3, Table 5", "known_uncertainty": "Whether the denominator must include only multiple-point faults (as used here) or also include other categories varies by interpretation. The form above follows Annex C.4 of ISO 26262-5:2018 to the best of my recollection." }, { "name": "PMHF (Probabilistic Metric for Hardware random Failures)", "expression": "PMHF = sum_{all violations of a safety goal} lambda_violation, where each lambda_violation is the failure rate of an item's hardware failure mode that violates a safety goal. Computed per safety goal. ASIL targets are expressed as an average PMHF in 1/h.", "variables": [ {"symbol": "lambda_violation", "meaning": "failure rate of a single hardware failure mode leading to violation of a given safety goal", "units": "1/h"} ], "worked_example": "Safety goal SG1: two contributing failure modes with lambda1 = 5e-9 /h, lambda2 = 3e-9 /h. PMHF_SG1 = (5 + 3)e-9 /h = 8e-9 /h = 8 FIT. ASIL C target: PMHF < 1e-7 /h (= 100 FIT average), ASIL D target: PMHF < 1e-8 /h (= 10 FIT average) per ISO 26262-5:2018 Table 5. 8 FIT satisfies ASIL D.", "authority": "ISO 26262-5:2018, Clause 8.3 and Table 5", "clause_locator": "ISO 26262-5:2018 Table 5", "known_uncertainty": "Targets in Table 5 are 'average probability per hour over the vehicle lifetime' expressed as order-of-magnitude bounds (e.g. <10^-7/h). The exact text 'average' vs 'instantaneous' should be verified in the standard." } ], "edge_cases": [ "Division-by-zero in SPFM/LFM when lambda_total = 0 (no faults considered): define metric = 1 (perfect) by convention, but flag explicitly.", "Threshold-exactly-equal semantics for ASIL targets: ISO 26262 uses strict inequalities (<) for PMHF and >= for SPFM/LFM; a calculator must NOT report 'meets ASIL D' when PMHF equals the threshold.", "Failure categories: if a fault is both single-point and multiple-point under different cuts, it is classified by the cut with the worst residual impact; dual classification must be rejected." ] }, { "family": "JEDEC FIT-rate math (accelerated-test Arrhenius + chi-square confidence)", "formulas": [ { "name": "Arrhenius acceleration factor", "expression": "AF = exp( (E_a / k) * (1/T_use - 1/T_acc) ), where AF multiplies the use-condition failure rate vs accelerated-test failure rate (lambda_use = AF * lambda_acc when AF > 1). Temperatures in Kelvin.", "variables": [ {"symbol": "AF", "meaning": "acceleration factor (dimensionless, use-condition failures per accelerated-test failure)", "units": "-"}, {"symbol": "E_a", "meaning": "activation energy of the failure mechanism", "units": "eV"}, {"symbol": "k", "meaning": "Boltzmann constant = 8.617333262e-5 eV/K", "units": "eV/K"}, {"symbol": "T_use", "meaning": "use-condition (field) junction temperature", "units": "K"}, {"symbol": "T_acc", "meaning": "accelerated-test junction temperature", "units": "K"} ], "worked_example": "E_a = 0.7 eV (typical for electromigration / interconnect wearout), T_use = 328.15 K (55 deg C), T_acc = 398.15 K (125 deg C). (1/T_use - 1/T_acc) = (1/328.15 - 1/398.15) = 0.003047 - 0.002512 = 0.000535 1/K. E_a/k = 0.7 / 8.617333262e-5 = 8123.0 K. Product = 8123.0 * 0.000535 = 4.346. AF = exp(4.346) = 77.2. So one failure at 125 C corresponds to ~77 equivalent device-hours at 55 C. If lambda_acc = 1000 FIT at T_acc, lambda_use = 77,200 FIT * (T_use/T_acc) adjustment may or may not be applied depending on the model (see uncertainty).", "authority": "JEDEC JESD85-series (Arrhenius reliability methodology); k is CODATA", "clause_locator": null, "known_uncertainty": "E_a = 0.7 eV is a generic rule-of-thumb for interconnect wearout; specific mechanisms have different values (dielectric TDDB ~0.3-0.5 eV, electromigration ~0.6-0.9 eV, solder fatigue ~0.5-1.0 eV). The E_a-to-mechanism mapping is mechanism-dependent and the Arrhenius form itself is not valid for all mechanisms (e.g. not for some mechanical fatigue regimes). The simple AF above does not include a voltage-acceleration term (e.g. E-model or 1/V model); for TDDB or hot-carrier, voltage acceleration must be multiplied separately." }, { "name": "Chi-squared point estimate of MTBF/FIT from zero failures in test time t", "expression": "FIT_observed = chi2(2*(c+1), CL) / (2 * t_devices_acc) in failures per device-hour. For c = 0 failures observed, t_devices_acc = (number of devices) * (accelerated test hours) * AF to convert to use-condition device-hours. chi2(2, CL=0.6) = 1.833, chi2(2, CL=0.9) = 4.605, chi2(2, CL=0.95) = 5.991 (degrees of freedom = 2*(c+1) = 2).", "variables": [ {"symbol": "chi2(p, CL)", "meaning": "chi-squared inverse CDF at probability CL with p degrees of freedom", "units": "-"}, {"symbol": "c", "meaning": "number of failures observed", "units": "count"}, {"symbol": "t_devices_acc", "meaning": "total accelerated-test device-hours = N_devices * t_acc_hours", "units": "deviceh"}, {"symbol": "CL", "meaning": "confidence level (e.g. 0.6, 0.9, 0.95)", "units": "-"} ], "worked_example": "N = 1000 devices tested for t_acc = 1000 h at T_acc = 125 C. AF = 77.2 (from prior example) so t_devices_use = 1000 * 1000 * 77.2 = 7.72e7 device-hours at T_use = 55 C. With c = 0 observed failures, at CL = 0.6: lambda_upper = chi2(2, 0.6)/(2t) = 1.833/(27.72e7) = 1.187e-8 /h = 11.87 FIT (upper bound at 60% confidence). At CL = 0.9: lambda_upper = 4.605/(27.72e7) = 2.98e-8 /h = 29.8 FIT. Note: this is the upper bound; the 'point estimate' is conventionally set to zero when c = 0 and only the bound is reported.", "authority": "JEDEC JESD85 / JESD74-style chi-squared confidence limits", "clause_locator": null, "known_uncertainty": "The 'point estimate' for zero failures is genuinely ambiguous; some references report 0/2t as a point estimate while others report only the upper bound at a stated CL. The degrees-of-freedom form (df = 2*(c+1)) is the standard 'failures-only' timing; a 'time-only' censored alternative uses df = 2c. The exact CL conventions and df used in JESD85 should be confirmed in the standard text before compliance use." } ], "edge_cases": [ "c = 0 with very small t: lambda_upper blows up; calculator should report 'insufficient data' below a minimum device-hours threshold (commonly N*t > 1e6 device-hours as a sanity floor).", "T_acc <= T_use: AF <= 1 by Arrhenius (test is not actually accelerated); calculator should flag as misuse.", "E_a = 0: AF = 1 regardless of T; treat as 'no thermal acceleration assumed' rather than dividing by zero." ] }, { "family": "EAR advanced-computing parameters (ECCN 3A090: TPP and performance-density)", "formulas": { "name": "Total Processing Performance (TPP)", "expression": "TPP = (Composite Theoretical Performance, 64-bit words) + (Composite Theoretical Performance, 32-bit words). 64-bit CTP_i = (F_i) * (n_64_i) * (r_64_i) per computing unit i; 32-bit CTP_i = (F_i) * (n_32_i) * (r_32_i). Total TPP = sum over i of CTP_i(64) + sum over i of CTP_i(32). Units: Weighted TeraFLOPS (WT). Threshold: a chip is controlled if TPP > 700 WT. Note: dual-issue and vector/SIMD factors and 'data length' factors are folded into the per-instruction throughput n and r as defined in Supplement 1 to Part 774.", "variables": [ {"symbol": "F_i", "meaning": "computational-unit i's operating frequency", "units": "Hz"}, {"symbol": "n_64_i", "meaning": "max 64-bit floating-point operations per cycle per unit i", "units": "FLOPs/cycle"}, {"symbol": "r_64_i", "meaning": "64-bit 'data length' / architectural factor applied per the supplement", "units": "dimensionless"}, {"symbol": "n_32_i, r_32_i", "meaning": "same definitions in 32-bit", "units": "FLOPs/cycle; dimensionless"}, {"symbol": "WT", "meaning": "Weighted TeraFLOPS = 1e12 weighted FLOPs", "units": "FLOPs (weighted)"} ], "worked_example": "Single computing unit at F = 2.0 GHz, n_64 = 2 FLOPs/cycle (FMA), r_64 = 0.9 (illustrative). CTP_64 = 2.0e9 * 2 * 0.9 = 3.6e9 = 3.6 GFLOPS-weighted. n_32 = 4 FLOPs/cycle, r_32 = 0.9. CTP_32 = 2.0e9 * 4 * 0.9 = 7.2e9 = 7.2 GFLOPS-weighted. TPP = 3.6e9 + 7.2e9 = 1.08e10 weighted FLOPs = 10.8 WT. This is well below the 700 WT control threshold. Multiply by 100 computing units of the same class to approach the threshold (TPP ~ 1080 WT, which exceeds 700 WT and places the chip under ECCN 3A090).", "authority": "EAR Part 774 Supplement No. 1 (Composite Theoretical Performance definitions); ECCN 3A090 in the EAR Commerce Control List", "clause_locator": "EAR Part 774, Supplement No. 1 (CTP definitions) and ECCN 3A090 (technical note paragraphs)", "known_uncertainty": "Exact current TPP threshold of 700 WT is reported here per public regulatory text; verify against the current EAR (the value has been updated over the years). The 'data length' factor r is mechanism-dependent and the supplement's exact tables must be used, not a guessed 0.9. Some prior revisions used a 32-bit/64-bit weighting different from 1:1; the current 1:1 summation is what I recall." }, { "name": "Performance density", "expression": "PD = TPP / (manufacturing process node area metric). Under the regulation I recall: PD = TPP / (units of 'square mm per transistor' or similar). Specifically, a chip is controlled if (a) TPP > 700 WT, OR (b) PD exceeds a stated threshold of 5 WT per mm^2 of 'manufacturing-process-limited area.' Calculation: PD_WT_per_mm2 = TPP_WT / Area_mm2. Note: the area denominator is computed from the 'process node limit' that the chip could be manufactured at, not necessarily the actual node used.", "variables": [ {"symbol": "PD", "meaning": "performance density", "units": "WT/mm^2"}, {"symbol": "TPP", "meaning": "Total Processing Performance", "units": "WT"}, {"symbol": "Area_mm2", "meaning": "manufacturing-process-limited die area at the relevant process node", "units": "mm^2"} ], "worked_example": "TPP = 1080 WT (from prior example). Suppose Area_mm2 = 200 mm^2. PD = 1080 / 200 = 5.4 WT/mm^2. If the regulation's PD threshold is 5 WT/mm^2, then 5.4 > 5 triggers control. Note: above 5 WT/mm^2 with TPP < 700 WT still triggers control; a calculator must check BOTH conditions.", "authority": "EAR ECCN 3A090 technical notes", "clause_locator": "EAR ECCN 3A090, technical note paragraph(s) defining performance density", "known_uncertainty": "Threshold of 5 WT/mm^2 is from my memory of the rule as originally published; current EAR text should be verified, as thresholds have been adjusted in revisions. The exact definition of the 'area' denominator (gross die, process-limited, functional-block-only) is a critical detail and I am NOT certain of the precise wording in the current regulation; [blocked]](streamdown:incomplete-link)